
  [1mA. Singleton with environment-detected sink.[0m One [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m that checks [38;5;153mtypeof window[39m at import time and installs the
  matching sink itself. Call sites do [38;5;153mimport { log } from './logger.js'[39m. Fewest files. The cost is testability:
  import-time environment detection is exactly the thing [38;5;153mnode:test[39m can't easily vary, so the browser policy (hashed
  usernames) would go untested — and that's one of the two rules you're adding the module to guarantee.

  [1mB. Pure core + factory + two thin entry modules.[0m [38;5;153msrc/logging/core.js[39m exports [38;5;153mcreateLogger({ sink, level, [39m
  [38;5;153musernamePolicy })[39m and a pure [38;5;153mredact(fields)[39m. [38;5;153mlogger.node.js[39m and [38;5;153mlogger.browser.js[39m each construct a preconfigured
  singleton with the right sink and username policy. Call sites import the entry module for their half. One more file
  than A. The core is pure, so [38;5;153mnode:test[39m covers redaction, level filtering, record shape, [3mand[0m the browser's hashing
  policy by injecting a fake sink — no browser needed.

  [1mC. Pub/sub core.[0m The logger emits records to N registered subscribers; console is one subscriber, a future remote sink
  is another. Fan-out to console [3mand[0m a collector comes free, which is the realistic end state once shipping exists. But
  it buys flexibility you don't need for one sink, and it raises questions you'd then have to answer — subscriber error
  isolation, ordering, backpressure — none of which have a forcing function yet.

  [1mRecommendation: B.[0m It delivers the sink seam you asked for at nearly A's size, and it's the only one where the
  redaction and username guarantees are actually covered by tests. C's fan-out is a real advantage later, but B's sink
  parameter can become a sink [3marray[0m when a collector exists — that upgrade is contained in the core, not spread across
  call sites.
